.rm 65
.bd
SUBROUTINE(S)
.in +5
.br
.bd
fmtstr
- format into a string
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
char *fmtstr(buf, fmt, ...)
.br
char *buf, *fmt;
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Fmtstr
writes the format
.bd
fmt
into
.bd
buf,
replacing each conversion with the argument that follows, and
terminates the result with a null.  It returns a pointer to that
null - to the end of what it wrote, not to the beginning - so that
successive calls can be strung together to build one string without
walking it again:

.sp
.in +5
p = fmtstr(buf, "%s:%d: ", file, line);
.br
p = fmtstr(p, "%s\n", msg);
.in -5
.sp

The conversions are
.bd
%s
for a string,
.bd
%d
for an int,
.bd
%ld
for a long,
.bd
%x
for an unsigned in lower case hexadecimal,
.bd
%c
for a character, and
.bd
%%
for a percent sign.  There is no field width, no precision and no
padding.

This is deliberately not
.bd
printf.
The compiler passes want a formatter that costs a few hundred bytes
and reaches no further than the string it is given; a program that
uses this one does not drag in stdio, the buffers or the cleanup at
exit.  It is the only entry point in
.bd
libccc.a,
which is the code cpp, c0, c1 and peep have in common.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ERRORS
.in +5
.br
Nothing is bounded.
.bd
Fmtstr
writes as much as the format and its arguments come to, and the
caller is responsible for a buffer that can hold it.  There is no
count argument and no truncation.

A conversion it does not know is dropped, and so is its argument:
the argument is not consumed, so every conversion after it takes the
wrong value.  Only the six above are understood.

.bd
%l
is assumed to be
.bd
%ld.
The letter after the l is skipped without being looked at, so
.bd
%lx
prints in decimal rather than hexadecimal, and consumes a long
either way.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
